arguments.content:
Root cause of the Mesa mmap failure is a missing 3.10 dma-buf feature, proven end-to-end:

```
FBDDBG mmap FAIL handle=5 size=18446744073709551615 (SIZE_MAX) off=0x10193000
```
Mesa's panfrost import does `bo->size = lseek(dmabuf_fd, 0, SEEK_END)`; our `drivers/base/dma-buf.c` `dma_buf_fops` has only `.release` and `.mmap` (**no `.llseek`**), so lseek fails and size becomes SIZE_MAX → `os_mmap(-1)` → ENOMEM before any syscall. Verified in the tree: `dma_buf_fops` at drivers/base/dma-buf.c:80-83.

**int86** = int85 + a small core patch (backport of upstream dma-buf SEEK_END support):

```c
static loff_t dma_buf_llseek(struct file *file, loff_t offset, int whence)
{
	struct dma_buf *dmabuf = file->private_data;
	loff_t base;

	if (whence == SEEK_SET)
		base = 0;
	else if (whence == SEEK_CUR)
		base = file->f_pos;
	else if (whence == SEEK_END)
		base = dmabuf->size;
	else
		return -EINVAL;

	return base + offset;
}
...
static const struct file_operations dma_buf_fops = {
	.llseek		= dma_buf_llseek,
	.release	= dma_buf_release,
	.mmap		= dma_buf_mmap_internal,
};
```

Gates: `dma_buf_llseek` in vmlinux, `.llseek` in dma_buf_fops, `lseek(exported_fd, 0, SEEK_END)` returns the BO size (test with a small userspace program or mesaprobe after flash), plus all int85 gates unchanged. Report shas; I flash and run mesaprobe/kmscube with mesa20-kmsro.

